== 9. Secret Duties Are the Most Soul-Enriching

Ninthly, consider that secret duties are the most soul-enriching duties. Just as secret meals make fat bodies, so secret duties make fat souls; and as secret trade brings in great earthly riches, so secret prayers make many rich in spiritual blessings and heavenly riches. Private prayer is that secret key of heaven that unlocks all the treasures of glory to the soul. God usually gives the best riches and the sweetest mercies to His people when they are in their closets on their knees. Just as the warmth that the chicks find by sitting close under the hen’s wings nurtures them, so the graces of the saints are enlivened and nurtured and strengthened by the sweet, secret influences that their souls come under when they are in their closet communion with God. Private prayer, conscientiously performed, is the secret key of heaven that has unlocked treasures and secrets that the craftiest devil has never had the skill to find out. Private prayer is the midwife that delivers the choicest mercies and the greatest riches to us.

Certainly, no one is so rich in gracious experiences as those who most exercise themselves in closet duties. “This poor man cried,” says David, “and the #smallcaps[Lord] … saved him from all his troubles” (Ps 34:6). David, pointing to himself, tells us that he “cried,” that is, silently and secretly, as Moses did at the Red Sea, and as Nehemiah did in the presence of the king of Persia. “And the #smallcaps[Lord] … saved him from all his troubles” (v. 6; Exod 14:15; Neh 1:11; 2:4). And oh, how much these deliverances added to his experiences! O my friends, the tender dew that falls in the silent night makes the grass and herbs and flowers flourish and grow more abundantly than great showers of rain that fall in the day. Just so, secret prayer will make the sweet herbs of grace and holiness grow and flourish in the soul more abundantly than all those more open, public, and visible duties of religion, which are too, too often mingled and mixed with the sun and wind of pride and hypocrisy.

Beloved, you#super[+] know that a favorite at court often gets more by one secret petition, by one private request to his prince, than a tradesman or a merchant gets by twenty years’ labor and pains. So a Christian often gets more by one secret petition, by one private request to the King of kings, than many others do by trading long in the more public duties of religion. O sirs, remember that in private prayer we have a far greater advantage for exercising our own gifts and graces and talents than we have in public; for in public we only hear others exercise their talents and gifts. In public duties we are more passive, but in private duties we are more active. Now, the more our gifts and talents and graces are exercised, the more they are strengthened and increased. All acts strengthen habits. The more sin is put into practice, the more it is strengthened. And so it is with our gifts and graces: the more they are exercised, the more they are strengthened.
